A||N||6||grinthock||Vac Line Replacement||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||11-28-2001||08:55 AM||||How does on replace a bad vac line?  More specifically where do I get a replacement?  Is there a universal and I just use a bender?  Or is there such thing as a bender, I have no idea what I am talkin about!<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||11-28-2001||11:33 AM||||Vac lines are all rubber hoses on a fiero I believe. Didn't you just get a new motor within the last year?? I would thinkt he vac lines would be fine. Unless you want to change the colour of them. There all rubber all you need is a Knife to cut it. Go to crappy tire and take what you need , it's cheap.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||11-28-2001||12:32 PM||||Well, on a V6, there are a wealth of hard, plastic vacuum lines.  They get very brittle with age and break easily.  I've used small sections of rubber vacuum hose to replace or patch broken plastic lines.  Rodney Dickman sells metal vacuum lines to replace all these under the plenum, for about $79, I think.  Not a bad idea.<P>You can get both hard plastic and rubber vacuum lines and fittings at most auto parts stores, too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Haagster||11-28-2001||01:14 PM||||G, I got a set of metal vac lines from Rodney Dickman and I must say they are OUTSTANDING.  They fit perfect, and are ideal replacements!!  Kudos to Rodney again.<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_8.jpg">   <BR>87GT Auto||||reg||6
Z||000004||Pyric||11-28-2001||01:39 PM||||Me too, I got the Stainless Steel ones in the mail yesterday and they are quite the high quality replacements.  <P>Better yet is the fact that I will not have to worry about a vacuum leak for a LONG time.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>"Never offend people with style when you can offend them with substance." <BR>--Sam Brown<p>[This message has been edited by Pyric (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||grinthock||11-28-2001||03:37 PM||||so I could just use rubber hose??  Instead of the hard plastic stuff?<P>Yes slammed it was rebuilt but the shop that rebuilt it refuses to look at the reasons why the car is running rich.. It wasn't before the rebuild, and has been since the day I picked it up..<P>Their claim?  The car is drinking gas because of the different TC we put in.<P>I find out a month later it's running rich, they claim something else died and it's not their problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||11-28-2001||06:30 PM||||I had used rubber lines to connect my broken lines, but eventually I just went with Rodney's.  Just a matter of removing the top manifold and the throttle body.  Took me about an hour to do.  My metal line for my EGR valve didn't quite want to bend right, so I just kept the plastic one in there since it was easier to make fit and it was still OK.  If I hadn't got the steel ones, they wouldn't have survived the new engine install.||||reg||1
